# ''Service owners'' — [[Tjenesteeiere]] [service owners] is there, but no service has a `tjenesteeier` [service owner] yet. Where do we get the list of services → owners? A spreadsheet is fine. Until then the owner column in the ToDo lists and the owner grouping under //Tjenester// [services] are empty.
# ''Data cleanup'' — can we normalise the spelling and casing drift in tags, such as `Ekstern tjeneste` [external service] / `Ekstern Tjeneste` and `Leveranse` [delivery] / `leveranse`? Which tag families first? [[Anomalier]] [anomalies] documents this and the other classes of problem, with the affected tiddlers, and which of them can be fixed mechanically. These changes will rewrite your data, so we want your approval per class.
# ''Archiving'' — [[Arkiv]] [archive] hides prior years behind an `Arkiv` tag, reversibly, without deleting anything. Is that enough, or should old years leave the live wiki entirely (a separate read-only archive wiki)?
# ''Business-review rules'' — the ToDo list counts a review as done only if its text differs from the template and from last month's review. Is an exact comparison right, or should a review that is a near-copy be flagged too? And should `Forretningsmessig endring` [business change, i.e. severity] be required on a new review? 84% of existing reviews lack it, so we made it optional.
# ''Which services should report?'' — the ToDo lists default to the services that have filed at least one review this year: 43 of the 60 services in the wiki have for 2026. The other 17 only appear under the //Alle tjenester// [all services] toggle, so a service that has never reported is never chased. Is that the right rule, or should the list of services expected to report be maintained explicitly?
# ''Permalink base'' — is `https://tiddlywiki.plattform.nhn.no/#…` correct?
# ''Access control'' (§3.8) — the brief asks, optionally, to separate //editors// from //readers// using Microsoft AD. This cannot be done inside the wiki: a TiddlyWiki plugin runs in the reader's browser, so anything it hides or locks can be bypassed by anyone who can load the page. Enforcement has to happen on the server, and there are two workable patterns:
#* ''Auth proxy in front of the server'' (recommended) — a reverse proxy on the hosting platform authenticates each user against AD (OIDC / SAML / ADFS) and passes the verified username to TiddlyWiki in a request header. The Node.js server already supports this out of the box (`authenticated-user-header`, plus `readers` and `writers` lists of who may read and who may save). Readers get a normal wiki where saving is refused; editors can save. No code changes needed.
#* ''Two deployments'' — the editable server for editors, and the static single-file build (as in this demo) published read-only for everyone else, regenerated on a schedule. Simplest, but readers see a snapshot, not the live wiki.
#* ''What we need from you'': who owns the hosting of `tiddlywiki.plattform.nhn.no` and can configure it; whether an AD-integrated auth proxy is already available on that platform (it usually is on an internal web platform); and whether the editor/reader split should be by AD group or by a named list. With those answered we can write the deployment guide as the §3.8 deliverable.
# ''Deployment'' — how do you want the plugins delivered: as plugin folders dropped into the existing Node.js wiki, or as a rebuilt wiki? And who installs updates?
